# The Thumb!
Tamil title: கட்டை விரல்!

Dronacharya could no longer bear the prince's complaints. Arjuna angrily said, “A mere hunter knows the rare science of weapons you taught exclusively for me. I trusted you and was deceived.” Matters reached the point where the bond between guru and disciple seemed liable to break.

“Come, let us go and see that hunter,” Drona said.

“See him and fall at his feet?” Arjuna mocked.

“I shall destroy the skill Ekalavya possesses. Come,” Drona replied firmly.

They went. The hunter saw Drona from far away. Arjuna stayed behind while the guru walked swiftly forward. Ekalavya bowed at his feet. Drona continued walking, and Ekalavya followed devoutly, crying, “Gurudeva! Gurudeva!” To Arjuna's eyes they became only two shadowy figures, so far had they gone.

Ekalavya once more fell at Drona's feet and rose. As if emerging from silence, Dronacharya looked up at him.

**Ekalavya:** Gurudeva, your servant Ekalavya salutes you.

**Drona:** Rise, son. You never asked me for lessons.

**Ekalavya:** I did not receive that fortune, master. But I installed you as Gurudeva in my heart and learned archery.

**Drona:** You have achieved rare mastery too. Arjuna will be... yes... delighted.

**Ekalavya:** It is your gracious blessing.

**Drona:** Ah, you have mastered verbal skill as well. Your ability is evident. Now your character must be tested.

**Ekalavya:** I shall be fulfilled, Gurudeva.

**Drona:** Why do you endlessly call me guru? I never instructed you for even one day, nor have you paid me even a small coin as an offering.

**Ekalavya:** Gurudeva, I am ready to give an offering. I waited only for the blessing of seeing you. Ekalavya is your servant. Whatever you command...

**Drona:** Ready to give? How boldly you speak! Many princes who come to study with me speak so. When they become kings, I remain myself while they are sovereigns.

**Ekalavya:** I am no prince, teacher. I am your slave.

**Drona:** Some princes have given what I asked because they possessed the means. Wealth lies at the feet of rulers. You, poor fellow, live in the forest.

**Ekalavya:** Yes, I am only a forest hunter. But my devotion to my guru surpasses that of rulers. Have not the slightest doubt, master.

**Drona:** Ekalavya, I commend your devotion. Live happily. I shall leave. What need have I to request anything from you?

**Ekalavya:** Must this sinner become fallen even after seeing you? Lord, accept me. Do not think a lowly hunter can give nothing. Though a hunter, I am a warrior bearing bow and arrow. I do not break my word or fail to accomplish what I resolve. Whatever you desire, I shall obtain.

**Drona:** True, through your archery...

**Ekalavya:** Not only that, teacher. My mind is pure.

**Drona:** Is that so? Poor man, you speak without knowing the world. I could ask an offering that would make you yourself say, “I cannot.”

**Ekalavya:** I who bear a bow will never say “I cannot.”

**Drona:** What did you say? Do not imagine this is weapon craft. The test of truth is exceedingly hard, and most fail it. Not everyone can act according to his word. Do not waste your promise in haste. I shall leave.

**Ekalavya:** Master, I do not know how to argue, nor would it be proper. But whatever offering you ask, I shall give it without fail.

**Drona:** You speak obstinately, madman. I can ask an offering that will leave you staring in terror. Asking is easy; giving is hard.

**Ekalavya:** Ask, Gurudeva. If I fail to give, none is lower than I.

**Drona:** You imagine I will ask for gold bars, nine gems or golden cloth.

**Ekalavya:** I have not thought of what you will ask, master. My only thought is that it must be said: “Gurudeva asked Ekalavya for an offering and received it.”

**Drona:** I warn you one last time. Do not kindle a flame you cannot endure.

**Ekalavya:** I am not afraid to walk the path of truth.

**Drona:** Then I ask my offering, witless babbler. Listen, tremble, lament, flee in terror, fall at my feet struck by shame and grief, beg forgiveness, and earn the name of a disciple unable to pay his guru.

**Ekalavya:** You still have not named the offering, Gurudeva.

**Drona:** I shall name it. A serpent will enter your ear. Listen: cut off the thumb of your right hand and give it as my offering.

**Ekalavya:** What shall I do? Alas, how can I give it? I would give all fourteen worlds, but how can I give my thumb, master?

**Drona:** Why, hunter-warrior, begin your lament!

**Ekalavya:** I shall return at once.

[He runs away and returns as promised with a water vessel.]

**Drona:** What is this vessel?

**Ekalavya:** To bathe your feet.

**Drona:** Why? Will you begin weeping after the ablution?

[Ekalavya washes Drona's feet. In an instant he cuts off his thumb and places it at them.]

**Ekalavya:** Here is the offering, Gurudeva: the thumb of my right hand. Master, blood has stained your feet. I shall cleanse them with this water.

Gurudeva, why? Are you tired or faint from standing so long? Alas! Lie down gently. It is only grass. Ah, you have fainted!

[Ekalavya sprinkles water upon Dronacharya's face and revives him.]

Ekalavya demonstrated the greatness of devotion to a guru and proved that he kept his word. From that day until now, everyone has praised his spirit of sacrifice. “Was he not the true disciple? Was that not devotion?” people marvel.

Very well. But while praising Ekalavya's devotion, should those connoisseurs, teachers and advocates of justice not also condemn Dronacharya's hard heart, deceitful purpose and despicable conduct; Arjuna's jealousy that incited him; and Drona's weakness in submitting to and pleasing Arjuna? As they praise the disciple's truthfulness, should they not condemn the guru's fraud?

From then until today, must the Ekalavya tale teach only how a disciple should behave towards a guru? Does it not also exemplify the conduct of those who served as teachers to royal families: gurus possessing the treacherous habit of pleasing whichever pupils came from wealthy families?

Did they present this lesson? Will they? Will they at least refrain from condemning us as villains and wretches when we do?

Ekalavya lost his thumb without committing any offence.

Drona demanded it as a guru's offering because Ekalavya's mastery of archery had made Arjuna jealous, and he wished to please Arjuna.

For a prince's heart to rejoice, that Aryan guru made a warrior of the hunter clan cut off his finger. Does this show only Ekalavya's devotion and not Drona's stony heart?

For ages teachers used this ancient story solely to explain the greatness of devotion. Today such stories help demonstrate how men disguised as sages in former times performed even dishonest and merciless acts without shame.

The people cannot be prevented from learning this new lesson. When they learn it, a storm must arise in their minds, and that storm cannot easily be suppressed.

Not only will no one today agree to be Ekalavya, no one will dare ask an offering like Drona. Gradually such stories will cease even to be included in curricula as incidents teaching morality. Even where they remain now, the teacher explaining them knows well what the students think upon hearing them.

One day this same story will inevitably be written to serve another idea:

There was once an archery teacher named Drona, a man of an exceedingly stony heart. A hunter named Ekalavya, amazed by his extraordinary skill, regarded him as guru, fashioned his likeness, and worshipped it. Ekalavya became a supreme archer. Learning this, Drona's disciple Arjuna was disgusted and rebuked his guru. To please him, Drona met Ekalavya, spoke cunningly, and obtained the thumb of his right hand as a guru's offering. Such deceitful and hard-hearted gurus lived in those times, and by many such treacherous acts they destroyed indigenous warriors like Ekalavya.

Will writers fail to tell it so?

Ekalavya's own clansmen had condemned his regarding Dronacharya as guru:

“However much desire you developed for archery, should you disgrace our people for it? We are hunters. Beasts and birds possess far greater skill than humans in escaping arrows, yet through our craft we kill them. Generation after generation we have mastered archery. From sparrow to lion, we have felled them with our bows. Born in such a people, do you learn archery from an Aryan, not even directly but by keeping an image? What have you done, Ekalavya? Archery is our hereditary vocation; Drona's people's vocation is mere grass lore. Born among hunters, how can you shamelessly call him your guru?

“However much fame and astonishing skill you obtain, what use is it? People will say it came through an Aryan's grace. Will the hunter world respect you? Will the Vedic crowd honour you?

“Ekalavya, I trusted that our clan would gain fame through your power, but you chose the indelible disgrace of becoming an Aryan's servant. Why did such a thought arise? Was there no one among us to teach you archery?”

A hunter chief might even have said this. Ekalavya ignored the sound counsel.

Finally, after losing his thumb and returning home, he encountered that chief and lowered his head.

“Ekalavya, I warned you at the beginning, but you did not listen,” the chief said with a breaking voice.

The warrior replied, “Do not grieve. I lost the thumb of my hand; he lost his honour. One day the world will understand.”

One could extend the tale at any length, saying that no hunter saw the severed thumb upon the ground without shedding tears.

One could write a play titled *The Thumb!* and stage it. Let fear seize the rulers!

Old tales will now yield only new lessons unless the authorities themselves “withdraw” them. The government will have to search for and ban plays and books one after another. As prohibitions multiply, the speed at which new ideas spread will also increase.

Fear that old stories will no longer be believed cannot be cured by polishing them, dressing them in modern philosophy, and setting them loose again. Others too can exercise the cleverness of giving those stories new clothes. Writers will inevitably retell them, as I have suggested, to reveal conjectures, consequences, inner meanings and mental dispositions. People will then lament in weariness: “The old story itself was tolerable; this man's new interpretation causes still greater trouble!”

---NOTES---
This essay and the separately archived drama `nadagangal/kattaiviral_1.md` are distinct canonical works despite their related title and subject. `Kattai viral` is the thumb. `Guru kanikkai` or `gurudakshina` is an offering owed to a teacher. The “fourteen worlds” is the traditional cosmological total.

The essay deliberately shifts from a dramatic reconstruction to direct criticism and then to an imagined future counter-narrative voiced by Ekalavya's hunter community. Caste descriptors and the opposition between “Aryan” and indigenous hunter are retained as the source's political language. The source provides no visible publication date on the recovered pages.
